<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"/><meta name="description" content="Sarah Whitaker's sports analytics research and large-scale data workflow."/><title>sports analytics research · sarahwhitaker</title></head>
<body class="night-design project-page"><AnimatedBackground/><main>
<header class="project-hero"><a class="back-link" href="/">♡ back home</a><p class="project-kicker">DATA & RESEARCH</p><h1>Sports Analytics Research</h1><div class="floral-divider" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><b>✿</b><span></span></div><p class="project-subtitle">data collection · cleaning · automation · validation · R · Python</p></header>
<article class="data-content">
<section class="data-hero"><p class="section-label">THE DATA</p><div class="big">2M+</div><h2>records analyzed</h2><p>This project was as much about building a reliable dataset as analyzing one. I worked with public data from multiple sources, automated collection, cleaned and standardized records, matched athletes across datasets, and validated the resulting data before statistical analysis.</p></section>
<section><p class="section-label">01 · DATA COLLECTION</p><div class="pipeline"><div><b>TFRRS</b><small>race & performance data</small></div><i>→</i><div><b>Athletic.net</b><small>athlete & performance data</small></div><i>→</i><div><b>MileSplit</b><small>public performance data</small></div></div><p>Rather than starting with one ready-made dataset, the workflow combined information from multiple public sources. The data had to be collected, organized, transformed, and checked before analysis.</p></section>
<section><p class="section-label">02 · AUTOMATION</p><div class="stats"><div><b>10,000+</b><span>public-source data points collected</span></div><div><b>Python</b><span>collection & organization</span></div><div><b>repeatable</b><span>automated workflow</span></div></div><p>Automating collection reduced repetitive manual entry and made large-scale data gathering practical.</p></section>
<section><p class="section-label">03 · CLEANING & MANIPULATION</p><div class="cards"><div><b>01</b><h3>Standardize</h3><p>Bring records from different sources into a consistent structure.</p></div><div><b>02</b><h3>Clean</h3><p>Handle inconsistent, incomplete, or unusable values.</p></div><div><b>03</b><h3>Transform</h3><p>Create analysis-ready variables and useful tables.</p></div><div><b>04</b><h3>Match</h3><p>Connect records representing the same athletes across sources.</p></div></div></section>
<section><p class="section-label">04 · MATCHING & VALIDATION</p><div class="validation"><div>raw records <b>→</b> matched athletes <b>→</b> validated records</div><p>Matching records across sources created another layer of quality control. The resulting fields and relationships were checked before the data was used in statistical analysis.</p></div></section>
<section><p class="section-label">05 · STATISTICAL ANALYSIS</p><p>Once the data had been collected, cleaned, manipulated, matched, and validated, I used RStudio to analyze the large dataset and develop statistical models. The preparation work was foundational because model results are only as reliable as the data going into them.</p><div class="tags"><span>RStudio</span><span>Python</span><span>data validation</span><span>statistical modeling</span><span>data visualization</span></div></section>
<section><p class="section-label">06 · TAKEAWAY</p><p>This project taught me that large-scale analysis starts long before the first model is fit. Building a useful dataset required automation, careful cleaning, record matching, validation, and repeated checks for unexpected results. Those same habits now shape how I approach quality and reliability in software.</p></section>
</article><footer><nav class="social-links" aria-label="Social links"><a href="https://github.com/sarahlouisewhitaker" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GITHUB</a><span>♡</span><a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/sarah-louise-whitaker" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LINKEDIN</a></nav></footer>
</main></body></html>
